{"id":"SPEC-003","spec_file_path":".claude/specs/michi-pipeline-cancellation.json","purpose":"Add cooperative, gate-only cancellation to michi-pipeline's execute_pipeline and execute_pipeline_parallel via a new, locally-defined CancellationToken type and a new ExecutionError::Cancelled variant, satisfying REQ-002's remaining cancellation-scoped done_when items. Cancellation is checked synchronously at existing per-step-attempt admission points only; it never reaches into a currently-running step's Step::run future to interrupt it. 'Bounded time' for already-in-flight steps to reach a terminal status is satisfied entirely by the existing per-attempt tokio::time::timeout inside CircuitBreaker::call (shipped in SPEC-001, unmodified by this spec) -- no new deadline or timeout mechanism is introduced. This spec touches crates/michi-pipeline only; it does not modify michi_core in any way (see the DomainError-mapping rationale in api_surface, which deliberately reuses an existing ErrorCode rather than adding a new one).","scope":"Additive to crates/michi-pipeline: (1) a new CancellationToken type backed by Arc<AtomicBool>, matching this crate's existing atomic-based synchronous-check house style (CircuitBreaker's phase and half_open_claimed fields are the precedent); (2) a new ExecutionError::Cancelled unit variant (the enum is #[non_exhaustive] and has already grown 6 to its current 9 variants across SPEC-001/SPEC-002 without incident); (3) breaking signature changes to both execute_pipeline and execute_pipeline_parallel, each gaining a new trailing `cancellation: &CancellationToken` parameter -- these are deliberate, acknowledged breaking changes to already-shipped public functions (michi-pipeline is pre-1.0 at version 0.1.0; this is the first breaking change either function has taken since SPEC-001/SPEC-002). execute_pipeline checks cancellation once per loop iteration, immediately before invoking the next step in its strictly sequential loop -- this is the only checkpoint in that function, and AC-010 pins its too-late-cancellation semantics precisely. execute_pipeline_parallel checks cancellation at spawn_ready_steps's admission point, evaluated each time the JoinSet driver loop is about to decide which Pending steps to spawn (including once before the very first spawn) -- this is the only checkpoint in that function, and AC-010a pins its too-late-cancellation semantics precisely, symmetric to AC-010. Once cancellation is observed at either function's checkpoint, every step still Pending is written to StepStatus::Skipped (reusing the existing StepStatus::Skipped variant and, in the parallel case, the exact same propagate_skip-adjacent Pending-to-Skipped write path SPEC-002 already built for dependency-driven skip propagation -- both mechanisms defer the actual PipelineStep.status field write to the same terminal sweep, so a step skipped for either reason is written exactly once, never double-written), no further spawns/attempts occur, and execute_pipeline_parallel continues draining the JoinSet -- awaiting every already-Running step to its own real terminal outcome -- before returning. No changes to michi_core::pipeline::Pipeline, PipelineStep, or StepStatus, and no changes to michi_core::ErrorCode or any other michi_core type.","non_goals":["Force-aborting a step's in-flight side effects on cancellation -- cancellation stops execute_pipeline from starting new step attempts, but does not reach into a currently-running step's Step::run future to kill work already dispatched, since michi-pipeline does not control what a step implementation does internally.","Resuming a cancelled run -- cancellation is terminal for that execute_pipeline invocation.","Cross-process or distributed cancellation -- the cancellation operation is scoped to a single in-process execute_pipeline invocation and its direct caller.","No changes to michi_core::pipeline::Pipeline, PipelineStep, or StepStatus -- this spec must not touch michi_core's pipeline module at all.","No changes to michi_core::ErrorCode or any other michi_core type -- ExecutionError::Cancelled's DomainError conversion (AC-022) deliberately reuses the existing ErrorCode::Unavailable variant with an explicit retryable(false) override, rather than adding a new ErrorCode variant, specifically to avoid touching michi-core's own internal exhaustive matches (ErrorCode::label() and ErrorCode::default_class(), both in crates/michi-core/src/error.rs, have no wildcard arm and would require updates if a new variant were added) and to keep this spec's blast radius within crates/michi-pipeline only, consistent with SPEC-001 and SPEC-002's precedent of never modifying michi-core.","Adding a new crate dependency (e.g. tokio-util's CancellationToken) -- CancellationToken is defined locally in michi-pipeline using Arc<AtomicBool>, matching this crate's existing atomic-based house style rather than pulling in a new dependency.","An awaitable `.cancelled()` future or any async cancellation-notification mechanism -- cancellation is polled synchronously at existing checkpoints only, never awaited as an event.","A new StepStatus variant for a cancelled-but-never-started step -- such steps reuse the existing StepStatus::Skipped. ExecutionError::Cancelled is a distinct, unrelated type describing the function's return value, not a new PipelineStep.status.","Any new deadline, timeout, or polling-interval configuration for cancellation responsiveness -- the existing per-attempt CircuitBreaker::call timeout (SPEC-001, unmodified) is what already bounds how long an in-flight step can run before its own attempt resolves.","Precedence between ExecutionError::StepFailed and ExecutionError::Cancelled is resolved by this spec (AC-017, mirroring SPEC-002's own AC-017, which already establishes that any Failed status overrides an otherwise-clean Ok(()) return -- this spec extends the same StepFailed-is-authoritative rule to override Cancelled too), but the relative ordering of which concurrently-completing step's failure is reported when several fail around the same time as a cancellation remains unspecified, mirroring SPEC-002's own no-ordering-guarantee stance for simultaneous step failures (that spec's AC-005).","Any internal checkpoint granularity finer than what AC-010 (execute_pipeline) and AC-010a (execute_pipeline_parallel) explicitly pin down -- those two criteria are the complete, caller-facing timing guarantee; no additional polling frequency or intermediate check is promised or should be assumed beyond them."],"api_surface":[{"name":"CancellationToken","signature":"#[derive(Clone)]
pub struct CancellationToken(std::sync::Arc<std::sync::atomic::AtomicBool>);

impl CancellationToken {
    pub fn new() -> Self;
    pub fn cancel(&self);
    pub fn is_cancelled(&self) -> bool;
}","description":"A cooperative, gate-only cancellation signal shared by cloning (cheap Arc clone) between a caller holding one instance and the in-flight execute_pipeline/execute_pipeline_parallel call it was passed into. new() constructs a token in the not-cancelled state. cancel() is idempotent and may be called from any task/thread at any time, including before the associated run starts or after it has already returned. is_cancelled() is a synchronous, non-blocking check -- there is no awaitable variant. CancellationToken implements Send + Sync (AC-021a), since Arc<AtomicBool> is Send + Sync and this is load-bearing for the realistic caller pattern of holding one clone on a spawning task while another clone is passed into the pipeline call."},{"name":"ExecutionError::Cancelled","signature":"/// Cancellation was observed before the pipeline run completed every step.
#[error("pipeline execution cancelled")]
Cancelled,","description":"A new unit variant (no fields) on the existing #[non_exhaustive] ExecutionError enum. is_retryable() returns false for this variant -- cancellation is a deliberate, terminal caller decision, not a transient failure worth retrying (AC-019). impl From<ExecutionError> for michi_core::DomainError's new arm for this variant constructs DomainError::new(michi_core::ErrorCode::Unavailable, "pipeline execution cancelled").retryable(false) (AC-022) -- ErrorCode::Unavailable is reused rather than adding a new ErrorCode variant (see non_goals for the rationale); the explicit .retryable(false) override takes precedence over Unavailable's own is_retryable_by_default()==true convention, exactly as the existing Http conversion arm already overrides its code's default via an explicit .retryable(...) call rather than relying on the code's default."},{"name":"execute_pipeline","signature":"pub async fn execute_pipeline(pipeline: &mut michi_core::pipeline::Pipeline, runners: Vec<Box<dyn Step>>, breaker: &CircuitBreaker, jitter_seed: f64, cancellation: &CancellationToken) -> Result<(), ExecutionError>","description":"Breaking change: adds a trailing `cancellation: &CancellationToken` parameter to the existing SPEC-001 signature. Before invoking each step in its strictly sequential loop, checks cancellation.is_cancelled(); if true, writes that step's (and every subsequent not-yet-attempted step's) PipelineStep.status to StepStatus::Skipped without invoking Step::run, and returns Err(ExecutionError::Cancelled). A step already in flight when cancel() is called elsewhere is unaffected and runs to its own real Completed/Failed outcome. This per-iteration loop-top check is the function's only cancellation checkpoint (AC-010 pins its too-late-cancellation timing precisely)."},{"name":"execute_pipeline_parallel","signature":"pub async fn execute_pipeline_parallel(pipeline: &mut michi_core::pipeline::Pipeline, deps: &StepDependencies, runners: Vec<Box<dyn Step>>, breaker: std::sync::Arc<CircuitBreaker>, jitter_seed: f64, cancellation: &CancellationToken) -> Result<(), ExecutionError>","description":"Breaking change: adds a trailing `cancellation: &CancellationToken` parameter to the existing SPEC-002 signature. At each admission checkpoint (including once before the first spawn_ready_steps call), checks cancellation.is_cancelled(); if true, writes every currently-Pending step's PipelineStep.status to StepStatus::Skipped and spawns no further steps, while the function continues draining the JoinSet until every already-Running step reaches its own real terminal status before returning. This admission-point check is the function's only cancellation checkpoint (AC-010a pins its too-late-cancellation timing precisely, symmetric to AC-010). Returns Err(ExecutionError::Cancelled) unless a step failure was also observed in the same run, in which case Err(ExecutionError::StepFailed) takes precedence (AC-017)."}],"acceptance_criteria":[{"id":"AC-001","criterion":"CancellationToken::new() constructs a token for which is_cancelled() returns false immediately after construction, with no cancel() call having occurred.","is_error_case":false},{"id":"AC-002","criterion":"Calling cancel() on a CancellationToken causes a subsequent call to is_cancelled() on that same token to return true.","is_error_case":false},{"id":"AC-003","criterion":"Calling cancel() a second time on an already-cancelled CancellationToken does not panic, and is_cancelled() continues to return true afterward (cancel() is idempotent).","is_error_case":false},{"id":"AC-004","criterion":"A CancellationToken produced via Clone shares cancellation state with its source: calling cancel() on either the original or the clone causes is_cancelled() to subsequently return true on both the original and the clone.","is_error_case":false},{"id":"AC-005","criterion":"Given a multi-step Pipeline running via execute_pipeline, if cancellation.cancel() is called (from another task) while some step's Step::run is already in flight (dispatched and awaiting resolution), that in-flight step's Step::run is not interrupted or aborted -- it runs to its own real completion (resolving Ok or Err) exactly as it would have absent cancellation.","is_error_case":false},{"id":"AC-006","criterion":"Given execute_pipeline, if cancellation is observed at the loop-top checkpoint before step i would otherwise be invoked, PipelineStep.status for step i and every subsequent not-yet-attempted step is written to StepStatus::Skipped (via the normal terminal-status write path, not necessarily an immediate field write at the moment cancellation is observed), and Step::run is never invoked for any of them (proven via a zero-call-count spy witness).","is_error_case":false},{"id":"AC-007","criterion":"In the same scenario as AC-006, every step at an index earlier than i whose PipelineStep.status had already become StepStatus::Completed or StepStatus::Failed before the cancellation checkpoint fired retains that exact status unchanged -- cancellation never retroactively overwrites an already-terminal step's status.","is_error_case":false},{"id":"AC-008","criterion":"Given a CancellationToken that already has cancel() called on it before execute_pipeline is invoked at all, execute_pipeline observes cancellation at its very first loop-top checkpoint (before step 0), writes every PipelineStep.status to StepStatus::Skipped, invokes zero Step::run calls across all supplied runners (proven via a SpyStep call-count witness of 0, mirroring the crate's existing edge_endpoint_absent_from_pipeline_is_rejected zero-invocation pattern), and returns Err(ExecutionError::Cancelled).","is_error_case":true},{"id":"AC-009","criterion":"Given execute_pipeline where cancellation is observed at any loop-top checkpoint before every step has reached StepStatus::Completed, the function returns Err(ExecutionError::Cancelled) rather than Ok(()) -- even in a scenario where every Step::run actually invoked resolved