If a JS caller declares `{type: "int"}` but omits `intVal` (both are legal TypeScript — every `*Val` field is `Option<...>`), the value silently becomes `0`/`0.0`/`false`/`""` instead of erroring. Worse: a misspelled type string like `"integer"` (instead of `"int"`) silently falls into the catch-all arm and DROPS a correctly-supplied payload entirely, since the `_ =>` arm never looks at any `*Val` field.

