{type:'int', intVal: undefined} -> renders "0"       (should throw)
{type:'float'}                  -> renders "0"       (should throw)
{type:'bool'}                   -> renders "false"   (should throw)
{type:'str'}                    -> renders ""        (should throw)
{type:'integer', intVal: 42}    -> renders ""        (should throw -- drops a present, valid 42)
```

`"null"` IS a legitimate, documented discriminant (see the doc comment on `JsToonValue.r#type` at line 57: `"str", "int", "float", "bool", or "null"`) and requires no payload — this must keep working exactly as today (`Value::Null` / `KvValue::Missing`). The bug is specifically: (a) a *recognized* type whose payload is absent should error, not default, and (b) an *unrecognized* type string should error, not silently discard a possibly-present payload.

